      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.286,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "QUADRATIC EQUATIONS"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.872,
            0.105,
            0.899,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "45"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.146,
            0.716,
            0.187
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{If} b^2 - 4ac = 0, then x = -\\frac{b}{2a} \\pm 0, \\mathrm{i.e.}, x = -\\frac{b}{2a} \\mathrm{or} -\\frac{b}{2a}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.206,
            0.663,
            0.244
          ],
          "content": "So, the roots of the equation $a x ^ { 2 } + b x + c = 0$ are both ${ \\frac { - b } { 2 a } } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.253,
            0.899,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, we say that the quadratic equation $ax^{2} + bx + c = 0$ has two equal real roots in this case."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.297,
            0.9,
            0.337
          ],
          "content": "If $b ^ { 2 }   -   4 a c   <   0$ , then there is no real number whose square is $b ^ { 2 }   -   4 a c$ . Therefore, there are no real roots for the given quadratic equation in this case."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.342,
            0.9,
            0.382
          ],
          "content": "Since $b ^ { 2 } - 4 a c$ determines whether the quadratic equation $ax^{2}+bx+c=0$ has real roots or not, $b ^ { 2 } - 4 a c$ is called the **discriminant** of this quadratic equation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.387,
            0.534,
            0.406
          ],
          "content": "So, a quadratic equation $ax^{2}+bx+c=0$ has"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.412,
            0.548,
            0.431
          ],
          "content": "(i) two distinct real roots, if $b ^ { 2 } - 4 a c > 0 ,$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.437,
            0.53,
            0.456
          ],
          "content": "(ii) two equal real roots, if $b^{2}-4ac=0,$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.105,
            0.462,
            0.458,
            0.481
          ],
          "content": "(iii) no real roots, if $b ^ { 2 } - 4 a c < 0 ,$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.488,
            0.46,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": "Let us consider some examples."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.519,
            0.901,
            0.558
          ],
          "content": "**Example 7:** Find the discriminant of the quadratic equation $2x^{2} - 4x + 3 = 0$ , and hence find the nature of its roots."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.564,
            0.901,
            0.603
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** The given equation is of the form $ax^{2}+bx+c=0$ , where $a = 2,   b = -4$ and $c = 3$ . Therefore, the discriminant"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.608,
            0.742,
            0.628
          ],
          "content": "b^{2}-4\\overline{ac}=(-4)^{2}-(4\\times2\\times3)=16-24=-8<0",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.635,
            0.482,
            0.654
          ],
          "content": "So, the given equation has no real roots."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.666,
            0.902,
            0.744
          ],
          "content": "**Example 8 :** A pole has to be erected at a point on the boundary of a circular park of diameter 13 metres in such a way that the differences of its distances from two diametrically opposite fixed gates A and B on the boundary is 7 metres. Is it possible to do so? If yes, at what distances from the two gates should the pole be erected?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.749,
            0.515,
            0.789
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** Let us first draw the diagram (see Fig. 4.2)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.794,
            0.517,
            0.911
          ],
          "content": "Let P be the required location of the pole. Let the distance of the pole from the gate B be x m, i.e., $\\mathrm { B P }   =   x \\mathrm { ~ m ~ }$ . Now the difference of the distances of the pole from the two gates = AP – BP (or, $\\mathrm{BP-AP} =$ 7 m. Therefore, $\\mathrm{AP} = (x + 7) \\mathrm{m}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.593,
            0.748,
            0.818,
            0.903
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.668,
                0.916,
                0.753,
                0.935
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.2"
            }
          ],
